  "full_review": "Anyone who has watched Aussie adventurer Steve Irwin’s show on the Animal Planet cable channel will have a pretty good sense of what the daffy action flick “The Crocodile Hunter: Collision Course” (MGM) is like. The main attraction here is the affable, nature-loving Irwin, who with his sidekick and wife Terri, get mixed up in a secret CIA operation when a crocodile they try to save accidentally swallows an electronic beacon from a missing spy satellite.\n\n����������� The film is goofy and silly, wrapping a slim story around wildlife expert Irwin’s high jinks with the animals of the Outback. The narrative has three parts that eventually collide (hence the title) in the final 10 minutes or so. After a spy satellite goes down, American CIA operatives are dispatched down under to retrieve the missing electronic beacon, which of course contains data that in the wrong hands could mean the end of the world.\n\n����������� In the meantime, ornery, gun-toting Aussie rancher Brozzie (Magda Szubanski) has been having a devil of a time with a mean 14-foot crocodile that has been gobbling up her cattle as well as scarfing down the CIA electronic beacon. Steve and Terri have been called in to rescue and relocate the croc before Brozzie fills it with lead, which makes them prime suspects for the CIA, which considers the nature duo international spies.\n\n����������� Director John Stainton strains to make the three parts meld into one. But he never forgets who the star of the show is. A good bulk of the time is spent watching “Stevo” rescue poisonous snakes, a bird-eating venomous spider, or a baby kangaroo after its mother is road kill. His over-the-top delivery of environmental babble is entertaining in an insane sort of way. As in the cable show, he speaks directly to the camera and has a tendency to talk to the camera when it may seem more sensible to focus on the beasts he’s got perilously close to him.\n\n����������� Irwin’s mad enthusiasm for all things natural is infectious. In between advising kids not to do this at home, he subdues a cranky killer serpent and he imparts simple messages about the need to keep the environment in balance and respect Mother Nature’s gifts. In his zeal to save the Earth, he wrestles cantankerous crocs and flings them into his tiny motorized boat to relocate them to a better place while wife Terri fumbles around trying to help. Her unable assistant persona is a hilarious match to his hysterical gusto. This is a man who wrangles with modern-day dinosaurs because he likes to, not because he has to. Throughout Irwin’s adventures, Sui, a poor old mutt who looks at her owner like he’s crazy (because he basically is), cringes and cowers in a corner of the boat while her master brings yet another ferocious thing with terrifying jaws on board.\n\n����������� There’s not much to “The Crocodile Hunter” but if the audience goes in expecting little, the reward is a good-natured film and a fun summer treat.\n\n����������� Because of some mild action violence and menace, the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops classification is A-II -- adults and adolescents. The Motion Picture Association of America rating is PG -- parental guidance suggested.\n\n______________________\n\n����������� Navarro is on the staff of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops' Office for Film and Broadcasting."
}